# Every Watch application configuration.
#
# Zephyr sources this file automatically for an application-level Kconfig; it
# must end by sourcing Kconfig.zephyr.

menu "Every Watch"

config EW_STAGE0
	bool "Build the stage 0 bring-up image instead of the application"
	default n
	help
	  Replaces the whole application with a minimal first-power-on image:
	  an I2C bus scan, a GPIO read, and a shell. No sensor drivers, no RTC
	  driver, no SPI, no BLE and no LED matrix, so there is nothing that can
	  hang during driver initialisation on an unproven board.

	  Enabled by stage0.conf, which is used as CONF_FILE rather than as an
	  overlay. Not useful on its own.

config EW_SELFTEST
	bool "Run the hardware self-test at boot"
	default n
	help
	  Probe every peripheral on the board and print a PASS/FAIL report to
	  the console before the normal application starts. Includes interactive
	  checks that need a person watching: an LED line and pixel-mapping walk,
	  and a left/right button press.

	  Intended for first board bring-up and end-of-line manufacturing test.
	  Requires a console backend — see bringup.conf (RTT, needs a debugger)
	  or bringup_usb.conf (USB CDC, no debugger needed).

config EW_SELFTEST_INTERACTIVE
	bool "Include the checks that need a person watching"
	depends on EW_SELFTEST
	default n
	help
	  Run the LED sweep, pixel-mapping walk, rainbow and button prompts as
	  part of the self-test. These take about a minute and need someone at
	  the bench, so they are off by default — with them disabled the boot
	  report covers only the automatic checks (I2C, RTC, sensors, GPIO) and
	  finishes in a couple of seconds.

	  Turn on for first bring-up of a new board or for end-of-line test.

config EW_SELFTEST_HALT_ON_FAIL
	bool "Stop before starting the application if the self-test fails"
	depends on EW_SELFTEST
	default n
	help
	  Leave the board sitting at the report instead of continuing to boot.
	  Useful on a manufacturing line where a unit must not be shipped if any
	  check failed. During bring-up you usually want the opposite: see the
	  failures but still get a running watch to poke at.

config EW_SAND_DEMO_AT_BOOT
	int "Seed the sand simulation at boot with this fill percentage"
	default 0
	range 0 60
	help
	  0 disables. Any other value seeds that percentage of the grid with
	  randomly coloured grains at the end of boot and leaves the display on,
	  so the sand simulation is running and visible without needing a
	  trigger.

	  Exists because the interactive `sand fill` shell command needs RTT
	  shell input, and OpenOCD's RTT server does not reliably deliver
	  writes to the target's down-buffer — the up-channel (log output)
	  works, the down-channel does not. This gives a way to see the
	  simulation on hardware regardless.

	  For bench and demo use. Leave at 0 for anything shipping: it holds
	  the display on indefinitely, which is neither the intended UX nor
	  kind to the battery.

config EW_IMU_ANYMOTION_WAKE
	bool "Arm the IMU any-motion trigger for wrist-tilt wake"
	default n
	help
	  Off because it does not work on this hardware. The BMI260 was tested
	  on 2026-08-02: with the display off and the board being shaken for
	  50 seconds the trigger fired zero times, while a stationary control
	  run was also zero. The driver reports success writing the feature
	  registers, so either the BMI260's microcode has no any-motion feature
	  or it lives at different addresses than the BMI270's page 1 0x3C/0x3E
	  that the code inherited — Bosch treats the per-part feature list as
	  NDA material, so the two cannot be distinguished from outside.

	  Arming it anyway costs a GPIO interrupt and some register writes for
	  a callback that never fires, and makes the boot log look like a
	  working wake path. Turn on only if the correct BMI260 feature-register
	  addresses are ever established — see src/imu/imu.c for what would
	  replace it (tap-to-wake, or software tilt detection).

config EW_BLE_TX_POWER_DBM
	int "BLE transmit power, dBm"
	default 0
	range -40 8
	help
	  Applied at runtime via the SoftDevice Controller's vendor HCI command,
	  per role, and the level the controller actually selected is logged.

	  REQUIRES CONFIG_BT_CTLR_TX_PWR_DYNAMIC_CONTROL=y, set alongside this in
	  prj.conf. That is what compiles the handler for the command into the
	  controller; without it the request returns -EIO, the radio stays at
	  0 dBm, and the only sign is an error nobody was logging. It cannot be
	  selected from here — Kconfig does not allow an int symbol to select.

	  Do NOT use CONFIG_BT_CTLR_TX_PWR_PLUS_8 for this. That Kconfig belongs
	  to Zephyr's open-source controller, and this build uses the SoftDevice
	  Controller — nothing in nrfxlib reads CONFIG_BT_CTLR_TX_PWR_DBM. Setting
	  it is accepted, appears in the generated .config as "=8", and leaves the
	  radio at 0 dBm. It looks applied everywhere except where it counts.

	  +8 dBm is the nRF52833's ceiling. The +9 and +10 options in Zephyr's
	  list require an external front-end module, which this board does not
	  have.

	  0 is the right default for a battery watch. Raise it only as a
	  deliberate trade, and remember it does nothing for reception — the
	  receiver is fixed-gain, so a connection still needs the other half of
	  the link to work.

config EW_BLE_CHANNEL_SURVEY
	bool "Measure RF energy on all 40 BLE channels"
	default n
	select BT_CTLR_SDC_QOS_CHANNEL_SURVEY
	select BT_HCI_VS_EVT_USER
	help
	  Turns on Nordic's QoS channel survey and logs the measured energy in
	  dBm for every BLE channel, roughly twice a second.

	  This is an antenna test that needs no reference transmitter, no known
	  distance and no second board, which is what makes it worth having when
	  every other measurement is guessing at what is nearby.

	  Read the profile, not the numbers. Wi-Fi occupies 2412, 2437 and 2462
	  MHz in 20 MHz blocks, so a receiver on a working antenna shows clear
	  humps over the corresponding BLE channels in any room with Wi-Fi. A
	  receiver whose antenna is not connected sees only its own thermal
	  noise: flat and featureless across all forty. Structure means the front
	  end is coupled to something; a flat line means it is not.

	  A value of 127 means no measurement for that channel. Survey work is
	  scheduled at low priority, so gaps are expected.

	  Bring-up only — it costs radio time and log bandwidth.

config EW_BLE_SCAN_DEBUG
	bool "Periodic BLE RSSI table (antenna health / signal-strength monitor)"
	default n
	help
	  Every 3 s, prints a table of every BLE advertiser currently heard:
	  address, name (if it carries one), latest and best RSSI, packet count,
	  and how many seconds since its last packet. Up to 16 devices, oldest
	  replaced first once full, so a long-running watch tracks what is
	  actually still around rather than filling up once and stopping.

	  Diagnostic for the case where bt_le_adv_start() reports success and yet
	  nothing can see the watch. Receive and transmit share the antenna and
	  matching network, so this cannot prove transmit works — but it can rule
	  the antenna in or out, which is the expensive thing to guess at. It also
	  works as a plain signal-strength monitor: watch the table update while
	  walking the watch around a room or away from a phone.

	  Read the strongest RSSI, not the count. A working antenna hears a phone
	  a metre away at roughly -50 to -60 dBm. Numbers down near -90 with a
	  handset alongside the watch mean the front end is barely coupled to
	  anything, and no amount of firmware will fix that. Silence in a room
	  with any Bluetooth in it says the same thing more bluntly.

	  Also switches scanning to active/100% duty cycle instead of the normal
	  passive/10% — see the scan_param comment in ble.c. Bring-up only: both
	  that and the table itself cost real power and RTT bandwidth.

config EW_IMU_AXIS_DEBUG
	bool "Log accelerometer axes, and keep the IMU alive with the display off"
	default n
	help
	  Bring-up aid for establishing the IMU_COL_AXIS / IMU_ROW_AXIS mapping
	  and their signs in src/imu/imu.c. Which way round the sensor sits on
	  the PCB cannot be derived from the source, and hardwarer_spec.md
	  records only the I2C address and the two interrupt pins, so the only
	  way to settle it is to hold the board in a known orientation and read
	  the vector back.

	  Two things happen when this is on. The raw vector and the sand
	  direction it produces are logged at 2 Hz, at INFO so no log-level
	  juggling is needed. And imu_suspend() stops parking the sensor when
	  the display goes off — otherwise a reading depends on getting a page
	  up first, which drags button handling and the display state machine
	  into what should be a direct measurement.

	  That second part means the accelerometer runs continuously, which is
	  exactly what the <10 uA idle target rules out. Bring-up only.

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
